{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 5, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words or Less\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—pink petals drifting like snow over temple roofs. I spent mornings sipping matcha in quiet tea houses, the bitterness balanced by the sweetness of wagashi sweets. At Kinkaku-ji, the Golden Pavilion shimmered above its mirror pond, untouched by crowds who’d yet to arrive.\n\nBy afternoon, I wandered Nishiki Market, where pickled radishes glowed like rubies and fresh tuna glistened under soft lights. I tried takoyaki for the first time—crispy outside, molten within—and nearly burned my tongue. A street vendor laughed and handed me a free cup of green tea. “Oishii?” he asked. “Oishii,” I replied, and he nodded like we’d just made a pact.\n\nTokyo was a whirlwind of neon and noise. Shibuya’s scramble crossing felt like being swallowed by a living organism. I got lost in Akihabara’s electric alleys, then found peace in the serene Meiji Shrine, where the rustle of bamboo leaves drowned out the city’s hum.\n\nOn my last day, I took the bullet train to Hiroshima. The Peace Memorial Park was quiet, solemn. A single origami crane, left by a child, rested on the cenotaph. I folded another, whispering thanks—for beauty, for resilience, for kindness.\n\nI left Japan with a suitcase full of tea, a notebook of haiku scribbled on receipts, and the quiet understanding that silence can be as loud as any sound.\n\nJapan doesn’t just welcome you—it changes you, gently, like the tide. I didn’t just visit. I breathed.\n\n(Word count: 298)", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2209511399269104}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.09506416320800781}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.31332796812057495}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.002152984729036689}, {"token": "3", "logprob": -0.0005376085755415261}, {"token": "0", "log